TLCacheCork: an opt-in to serve ReleaseAck ahead of GrantData on the inner D channel.

WHY.  TLCacheCork answers an InclusiveCache's clean Release (every eviction of a clean
victim) with a locally generated ReleaseAck on the SAME inner D channel that carries the
outer GrantData, through TLArbiter.lowestIndexFirst with the data first.  A cycle-accurate
simulation of this repo's generated L2 + cork RTL (fpga/pynq-z2/rtl_study/l2tb, calibrated
at one transaction in flight only) reproduces the silicon DRAM plateau and its dip at four
in flight, and attributes both to this arbitration: under a dense miss stream ReleaseAcks
wait ~7.3 cycles per block behind data, the cork's 2-entry ReleaseAck queue fills, it
refuses further Releases, the cache cannot schedule them, and every Acquire -- which may
only follow its Release -- goes out late and in clumps while the outer D sits idle.  In the
same simulation, ReleaseAck-first lands exactly on 64 B / 9 messages = 7.11 B/cycle.
fpga/pynq-z2/docs/MEMORY_BANDWIDTH.md section 6.

WHAT.  A CDE Field, TLCacheCorkReleaseAckFirst, default false.  When true the ReleaseAck
queue is the arbiter's first input; when false the arbiter call is textually the original,
so every config that does not set it elaborates byte-identically (checked against the
vendored PynqZ2RocketBigLittlePextTacitMicRgbBwConfig bundle).  A Field rather than a
TLCacheCorkParams member because the InclusiveCache coherence manager constructs the cork
with default params; reading p() inside the cork needs no change to that generator.

Applied with `git -C $CHIPYARD_DIR/generators/rocket-chip apply` alongside the other
*-rocketchip-* patches (scripts/07_patch_rocketchip.sh).

--- a/src/main/scala/tilelink/CacheCork.scala
+++ b/src/main/scala/tilelink/CacheCork.scala
@@ -17,6 +17,14 @@
 
 import freechips.rocketchip.util.DataToAugmentedData
 
+/** Serve the cork's locally generated ReleaseAcks AHEAD of the outer GrantData on the inner
+  * D channel.  The default arbitration is data-first; under a dense miss stream through an
+  * InclusiveCache that starves ReleaseAcks, fills the 2-entry ReleaseAck queue, stops the
+  * cache scheduling Releases, and so delays every Acquire behind them (iiswc-tutorial
+  * fpga/pynq-z2/docs/MEMORY_BANDWIDTH.md section 6).  Opt-in: false leaves the arbiter, and
+  * the generated Verilog, exactly as they were. */
+case object TLCacheCorkReleaseAckFirst extends Field[Boolean](false)
+
 case class TLCacheCorkParams(
   unsafe: Boolean = false,
   sinkIds: Int = 8)
@@ -169,7 +177,11 @@
 
         // Combine the sources of messages into the channels
         TLArbiter(TLArbiter.lowestIndexFirst)(out.a, (edgeOut.numBeats1(c_a.bits), c_a), (edgeOut.numBeats1(a_a.bits), a_a))
-        TLArbiter(TLArbiter.lowestIndexFirst)(in_d,  (edgeIn .numBeats1(d_d.bits), d_d), (0.U, Queue(c_d, 2)), (0.U, Queue(a_d, 2)))
+        if (p(TLCacheCorkReleaseAckFirst)) {
+          TLArbiter(TLArbiter.lowestIndexFirst)(in_d,  (0.U, Queue(c_d, 2)), (edgeIn .numBeats1(d_d.bits), d_d), (0.U, Queue(a_d, 2)))
+        } else {
+          TLArbiter(TLArbiter.lowestIndexFirst)(in_d,  (edgeIn .numBeats1(d_d.bits), d_d), (0.U, Queue(c_d, 2)), (0.U, Queue(a_d, 2)))
+        }
 
         // Tie off unused ports
         in.b.valid := false.B
